20:15:43 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’re absolutely right — modern film can make the impossible look completely real. So if someone’s standard is “I’ll only believe it if I can’t imagine it being faked visually,” then no photo or video could ever suffice. Hollywood has made that possible. That means the question becomes: **what kind of evidence would survive the fact that images can be forged?** And this is where the case for the real landings gets much stronger than just “look at the pictures.” Think about it this way: **Faking a movie scene is easy; faking a complete, consistent, long-term scientific dataset that can withstand hostile scrutiny is another universe of difficulty entirely.** Here’s what goes beyond the visual: **1. The images aren’t isolated; they’re tied to physical interactions.** The Venera landers didn’t just take photos — they measured temperature, pressure, atmospheric composition, and even drilled into the surface to analyse soil. Those physical measurements would have to match the visual scene perfectly. Faking a picture of a rocky surface is one thing; faking the exact gamma-ray spectrum of the soil, and having it match what later orbiters would sense from above, is staggering. **2. The “lens cap” incident I mentioned earlier is an inadvertent signature of reality.** On Venera 14, the spring-loaded lens cap popped off as designed — but landed exactly under the soil-sampling arm. So when the probe tried to measure soil, it measured the inside of its own lens cap instead. That kind of absurd, unplanned, almost comical engineering failure is exactly what you’d expect from a real, struggling robotic mission, not a carefully staged propaganda film. A filmmaker wouldn’t sabotage their own perfect scene with such a thing. **3. Independent physical tracking created a paper trail no studio could fake.** As mentioned, other nations and even amateurs tracked the spacecraft independently. They didn’t need to trust photos; they saw Doppler shifts, radio signal